OpenGL Utility Toolkit (GLUT) distributionVERSION: 3.7 (January, 2001)Mac OS Release Notes:This version implements numerous changes from the previous (GLUT 3.6 released with Apple OpenGL 1.0) version.  These include:- GamemodeGamemode is implemented as defined in the GLUT 3.7 specification.  Thismode uses the DrawSprocket library and thus must have it installed in the system, a GLUT fatal error is produced otherwise.  To implement gamemodeinclude the following:glutGameModeString("XDIMxYDIM:BPP");glutEnterGameMode();Where XDIM is the requested x dimension, YDIM is the requested y dimensionand BPP is the requested bit depth.  The string can also include "@HZ",where HZ is the requested refresh frequency.  In Mac OS GLUT this last parameter is currently ignored.  The string can have the partial form of"XDIMxYDIM" or ":BPP:" if desired in these case GLUT with try to find a suitable context with the current screen dimensions and maximum displaybit depth respectively.  In most cases GLUT will selectively reduce the requirements to find a suitable context rather than immediately failing.GLUT will reduce the size of window request until it fits in a 640x480 display and reduce the depth incrementally to 8 bits per pixel. Obviously,if a suitable context cannot then be found GLUT will fail.A program originally designed for windowed mode may want to assume the form:GLboolean fullscreen = GL_TRUE; glutInit(&argc, argv);if (argc > 1 && !strcmp(argv[1], "-w"))	fullscreen = GL_FALSE;//must init the display mode firstglutInitDisplayMode(GLUT_RGBA | GLUT_DOUBLE | GLUT_DEPTH);if (fullscreen) {  glutGameModeString("400x400:32");  glutEnterGameMode();}else {  glutInitWindowSize(400, 400);  glutCreateWindow("GLUT Window");}// continue with additional main code- Contextual MenusMenus in GLUT are now implemented as contextual menus.  These can be addedas follows:glutAddMenuEntry("Quit", 1);glutAttachMenu(GLUT_RIGHT_BUTTON);Note, glutAttachMenuName is no longer supported as it is no longer needed.The menu bar is still built and contains the Apple menu and the File menuwith the Quit item.  This ensures Cmd-Q is always supported and users canexit even if no menus are created in the window mode.The menus attach to the three possible mouse buttons, which are activatedas follows;click: left buttoncontrol-click: right buttonoption-click: center button- Command Line DialogThe command line dialog is no longer shown by default.  To enter commandline options at program start up, hold down the Command key when launching the application.  This allows a nicer user interface while still allowing access to the command line options.- Resource DependenciesGLUT is less resource dependent than before, but still requires "glut.rsrc"for both the about box and cursor support.- Renderer SelectionAdded AGL_ALL_RENDERERS and AGL_NO_RECOVERY to facilitate getting a hardwarerenderer on lower end and old systems.  If this poses a problem with compliancein your application, recompile with these (lines 120 and 121):attrib[nattrib++] = AGL_ALL_RENDERERS;attrib[nattrib++] = AGL_NO_RECOVERY;lines removed from "glut_dstr.c".- Sub-WindowsNot implemented as true sub-Windows but now sahould behave much better with respect to their parent windows- Joystick SupportNot yet implementedUpdated: (24 January 2001)